findings.recommendation:
Keep the test and its mutant-killing assertions; make the residual state explicit instead of implying it away. Rename to `a board missing a group does not throw (the missing group is not recreated)` and add two assertions after the existing ones:

  const result = migrateData(data);
  expect(result.boards[0].groupOrder).toEqual([
    'completed', 'backlog', 'focus', 'inProgress', 'orgIntentions', 'delegated',
  ]);
  // documented residual: sanitizeGroupOrder always returns a full permutation, but migrateData
  // never recreates a missing group — so a groupOrder id may name a group that does not exist
  expect(result.boards[0].groups.orgIntentions).toBeUndefined();
  expect(result.boards[0].groupOrder).toContain('orgIntentions');

While there, collapse the double invocation (see the minor below) so these assertions read off a single call. Then record the cross-task consequence in the decisions log and flag it to Task 07: either Task 07 filters groupOrder to ids present in board.groups before rendering, or it accepts that a hand-deleted group key crashes the view. Do not fix it by recreating groups inside migrateData — that is hardening beyond this task's scope, which the task's implementation hints forbid. No mock changes; this is a pure function on plain data.

Extend the existing `groupOrder null → default order` test with the identity assertions the sibling tests already use. Verified to pass on current code and to fail on the GROUP_IDS-reference mutant:

  it('groupOrder null → default order', () => {
    expect(() => sanitizeGroupOrder(null)).not.toThrow();
    expect(sanitizeGroupOrder(null)).toEqual(DEFAULT_ORDER);
    expect(sanitizeGroupOrder(undefined)).toEqual(DEFAULT_ORDER);
    // the default path must allocate, not hand out the exported constant
    expect(sanitizeGroupOrder(null)).not.toBe(GROUP_IDS);
    expect(sanitizeGroupOrder(null)).not.toBe(sanitizeGroupOrder(undefined));
    const result = migrateData(makeV8Data({ groupOrder: null }));
    expect(result.boards[0].groupOrder).toEqual(DEFAULT_ORDER);
  });

Add the two-board form as well, so the guarantee is pinned end-to-end on the path the v7 test cannot reach — a v8 input with two boards that both have `groupOrder: null`, asserting `result.boards[0].groupOrder).not.toBe(result.boards[1].groupOrder)`. No mock changes.

Pin purity with two lines in the same test, so it costs nothing:

  it('groupOrder already valid and non-default → preserved as is', () => {
    const custom = ['completed', 'delegated', 'orgIntentions', 'inProgress', 'focus', 'backlog'];
    const input = [...custom];
    expect(sanitizeGroupOrder(input)).toEqual(custom);
    // the sanitizer is pure: the caller's array is not touched, and the result is a new instance
    expect(input).toEqual(custom);
    expect(sanitizeGroupOrder(input)).not.toBe(input);
    const result = migrateData(makeV8Data({ groupOrder: [...custom] }));
    expect(result.boards[0].groupOrder).toEqual(custom);
  });

Add the same `expect(input).toEqual([...])` shape to one damaged-input case (e.g. the duplicate-collapsed test) so purity is pinned on the branch that actually rewrites the array. No mock changes.

Capture the result once, which also gives the round-1-major fix a single object to assert against:

  const result = migrateData(data);
  expect(result.boards[0].groupOrder).toEqual([
    'completed', 'backlog', 'focus', 'inProgress', 'orgIntentions', 'delegated',
  ]);

Keep a no-throw guarantee in the test by wrapping that single call: `let result!: PluginData; expect(() => { result = migrateData(data); }).not.toThrow();` — one invocation, both contracts asserted. No mock changes.
